            Written: @spec.Conformance (`workflow/specs/conformance/Conformance.spec`), status proposed, with 
            skill:to-spec.
            11 Requirements, 30 Invariants, 23 tests, 7 open questions.

            **Location changed.** The description says next to the suite; the user has since ruled that Specs live in 
            the
            workflow folder ("it should just be in the workflow repo", 2026-11-09). README work list item 7 now points
            there.

            **What it guarantees** (derived from the suite's purpose and the README's decisions, not from today's code):

            - R1 to R3: the Port carries public behaviour only (one settling exception, `pending`); verdicts never 
              depend
              on which runtime runs; adapters translate and never fill reports in from suite facts, outside stated 
              limits.
            - R4, R5: oracles anchor reports to suite facts; every case and oracle cites core Spec rules that resolve;
              no expected value comes from running a runtime.
            - R6, R7, R10: every check (an oracle, or a case expectation that alone checks an Invariant) has a mutant
              killed by that check; null fails every case by a stated failure; falsify's pass conditions.
            - R8: verdicts are a function of code, case and world seed; order and process split change nothing.
            - R9: one verdict per requested case, machine and human forms, exit codes.
            - R11: the old-suite comparison: the new suite kills every mutant the old one kills on ported cases; every
              difference has a reading.

            **Agent decisions for the user's read** (pending): a consistency-only check is never an Invariant's only
            check (R4); a thrown defect does not count as failing on null (R7); a separate `Defect` verdict kind,
            proposed name (R9).

            **Finding.** R7 does not hold today: `suite --runtime null` gives 0/61, but 29 of those failures are a
            thrown `NoHeldRequest`, which `dsl/case.ts` calls a scenario bug, never a verdict (Q2). The P3 evidence
            "null 0/61" overstates what null proves.

            **Tests:** 9 name existing checks (falsify's oracle-has-a-mutant, control, judge, exactly-once and null
            lines; `runCase`'s seeds; `report`; `sliceVerdicts`; the Oracle type); the rest say "phase 3 writes this"
            or "phase 4 writes this".

            **For skill:check-spec:** whether any Invariant restates current behaviour rather than purpose (the
            Task's false pass); whether R6's definition of a check (oracles plus case-only Invariants) is the right
            granularity, given Q5 (per-rule mutants); whether R2's "stated limit" escape hatch is bounded enough;
            whether `derives_from [@task.V3.T4]` is the right provenance, since no Plan outcome covers the suite.

            **Sidecars:** none started. The check and mutant inventory was read directly (4 oracles, 25 mutants in
            `oracles/*.lies.ts`) and the null run done by the lead, so no sidecar result to log.

            Gates: `workflow index`, `workflow format --write`, `workflow check` valid, `workflow refs --dangling` none.
        comment 2026-11-09T16:29:48Z
            **skill:check-spec, independent (Opus lead, 4 Haiku sidecars), at `4420d200`.** Contract: **ISSUES**. Design: **NOT APPLICABLE** (no implementation design; R6's kill rule is falsify's observable behaviour, judged as contract). Acceptance ("no gap in its contract") is not met yet.

            **Restating current behaviour (the false pass): mostly not.** Many Invariants demand what the code does not do (I17 hash, I18/I25 defect kind, I19/I20 compare, I29 compare mode, I9/I10 resolver, R1 JSON). Code-shaped wording to lift to purpose: R8 "(1 and 2)" (Conformance.spec:109, run.ts:7) → "at least two fixed seeds that change load order and session seeds"; I21 "first seed that failed" (:252) → "a failing seed"; R11/I29 "round 14" (:146, :271) → "the old suite's runtime"; R10's five mutant labels (:135-136) mirror main.ts:187-197.

            **Contract gaps, with fixes:**

            1. No Requirement that the suite judges the core Specs at all: R1 to R11 all hold for a suite with one oracle. Sources: P3.task:10 ("one oracle per guarantee"), :230-371 ("Every X Invariant has its oracle or case"). Fix: add R12, every core Spec Invariant in scope is judged by an oracle or a case expectation, or named as excluded with why; an Invariant that the guarantee map resolves against `workflow/specs/core/` and lists every Invariant; a test comparing them. This also defines "case-only Invariant" (:172, :232, undefined in `model`).
            2. Case-only mutants cannot be judged as written: R9 names no check for a case's own expectation (:116-117), yet R6 (:86-87) and I13 (:232) require the kill "by that expectation"; a defect or another expectation would count (run.ts:14-20). Fix: R9 names the failed expectation (label and cited rule); I16 judges by it.
            3. Check granularity (Q5): per-oracle lets a rule die unseen (README stated limit on `attempt-identity`). Fix: a check is an (oracle, Spec Invariant it names) pair, caught only by a violation naming that Invariant (violations already carry it, lane-cells.ts:259-266); per-rule isolation inside one Invariant stays an audit/`--sweep` item. Also R10 (:139) and I28 (:269) omit I13, so falsify can pass with case-only Invariants unmutated: add I13 to I28 and to R10's coverage (:313-315).
            4. R8 contradicts R9: a verdict is a function of code, case and seed (:105-107), but a time-bound overrun is a failure verdict (:118-119; wall clock, child.ts:57-61). Fix: an overrun is a defect (R9's proposed kind), never a stated failure; I19 holds for runs within the bound.
            5. R2's stated-limit exception is not bounded (:37-38). R2 does not require a limit to name its cases and checks (I4 does, :209-210), does not confine limits to the prototype adapter (only R3 does, for substitutes), and lets a limit loosen a check to accept any value (three-channels.ts:133-136, `onNone: () => true`) or change an expectation away from its cited rule (README:210-212, idle row vs Reads R14, against R5). Fix: a limit belongs only to a prototype adapter; it names the cases, the checks and the retiring behaviour; it may only skip a report the runtime does not make, never accept a contradicting value; an expectation that differs from its rule is a limit naming the case. Add an Invariant that the production adapter has none.
            6. Mutant containment: R6 (:89) and I17 (:241) protect only the runtime's tree, but the copy links `prototypes/conformance` to the original (falsify.ts:45) and `applyEdit` has no containment (falsify.ts:61). Fix: "a mutant's edits stay inside its copy; nothing outside it changes".
            7. R9's "names the checks, the seed, the step" (:116-118) has no Invariant (I24-I27); the verdict has no seed or step field (verdict.ts:5-14; a mismatch has no step, run.ts:14-17). Only the first failing seed is reported (run.ts:54-58), so a mutant red by a mismatch on seed 1 and its oracle on seed 2 reads "red by another check": R6 should judge over every failing seed.
            8. R1 "Every value crossing it is JSON" (:21) is contradicted by `Boot`'s hosts and `observe` (port.ts:189-195) and `Option` fields; README decision 11 says the Runtime's values. Fix: every data value (payloads, inputs, keys, memory, rows, Observation fields) is JSON; the world's hosts are the only functions. No Invariant covers this clause (I1 covers names only).
            9. Probable unrecorded violation of I8: WriteLanes I9 is judged only by lane-cells' `bounds` (lane-cells.ts:250-269), which reads only reports, and no case cites WriteLanes I9. Add it to the open list.
            10. T15's "Exists" half cannot fail on I18: main.ts:163-166 counts every `Fail`, including `threw:` defects (run.ts:20), so a null that throws on boot still prints "ok". Q2 records the shortfall; T15 should not claim it as existing.
            11. Smaller: I25 cites @R7 but R7's row lists only I18 (:301-303); R11's "a new-suite bug is fixed before the area counts as ported" (:149-150) has no Invariant.

            **Writing:** `basis` logs changes in STATUS.md (:7-8); to-spec and the core Specs use `workflow/specs/spec-log.md` (to-spec SKILL.md:40-41, WriteLanes.spec:4). Mutant verdict names differ across R6 (:87-88), R10 (:135-136) and `model` (:197-198). "29 of 61" is stated three times (:98, :128, :157). Run results in tests (T9 :341-342, T13 :348-349) belong in Task evidence (check-spec recording.md:11-12); "round 5's parity" and "rounds 1 to 3" are session residue.

            **Author's points.** `derives_from [@task.V3.T4]`: allowed (to-spec spec-author.md:36); add @plan.Runtime's verification target once the user approves that Plan edit (P3.task:21). Agent decisions: agree with all three. R4 (consistency-only never alone) follows policy-testing's "excuse only what the harness observed", though gap 9 suggests it fails today. R7 (a thrown defect is not a null failure): a defect reached no check. R9 (`Defect` kind): needed to make R7 and gap 2 checkable; extend it to overruns (gap 4).

            **Sidecar log.** (a) faithfulness: accepted the coverage-completeness, R1 JSON, R2 to R11 clause and I25 findings; rejected "policy quotes absent" (both are in policy-verification) and the speed targets, `nub run verify` and the coverage record as Spec gaps (they are P3 exit criteria and belong in the Plan record). (b) testability: accepted the null gate, first-seed judge, symlink escape, I8 and T5/T1 limits after reading the code; rejected the overlapping-occurrence edge as not verdict-changing; noted the non-parallel load failure with no per-id verdicts (main.ts:232-243) as a code violation of I24, not a Spec gap. (c) restating: accepted the seeds, round-14, labels and first-seed items; kept the `VERDICTS` line format, since the parent process parses it. (d) house shape: accepted the change-log location, name drift, repeated count and run results; rejected trailing spaces and continuation indents (formatter output, matching core) and "round 14" as residue (it names the runtime).
